	Difference between predicate and bi-predicate
	
	Predicate<T> → takes 1 input, returns boolean.
	BiPredicate<T, U> → takes 2 inputs, returns boolean.
	
	BiPredicate in Java
	
	1. What is BiPredicate?
	-----------------------
	- BiPredicate<T, U> is a functional interface introduced in Java 8, available in java.util.function package.
	- It represents a predicate (boolean-valued function) of two arguments.
	- Its single abstract method is:
	    boolean test(T t, U u);
	- It is often used when you need to compare or validate two inputs and return a true/false result.
	
	2. Key Points:
	--------------
	- Predicate<T> → 1 input, returns boolean
	- BiPredicate<T, U> → 2 inputs, returns boolean
	- It can be combined with other BiPredicates using:
	    - and()
	    - or()
	    - negate()

	3. Examples:
	------------
	Example 1: Check if first number is greater than second
	--------------------------------------------------------
	BiPredicate<Integer, Integer> isGreater = (a, b) -> a > b;
	System.out.println(isGreater.test(10, 5)); // true
	
	Example 2: Check if string starts with given prefix
	----------------------------------------------------
	BiPredicate<String, String> startsWith = (str, prefix) -> str.startsWith(prefix);
	System.out.println(startsWith.test("Java", "Ja")); // true
	
	Example 3: Combining BiPredicates
	---------------------------------
	BiPredicate<Integer, Integer> greater = (a, b) -> a > b;
	BiPredicate<Integer, Integer> equal = (a, b) -> a.equals(b);
	System.out.println(greater.or(equal).test(5, 5));  // true
	System.out.println(greater.and(equal).test(10, 10)); // false
	
	4. Real-world use case:
	-----------------------
	BiPredicate<String, String> validateCredentials = (username, password) -> 
	        username.equals("admin") && password.equals("1234");
	
	System.out.println(validateCredentials.test("admin", "1234")); // true
	System.out.println(validateCredentials.test("user", "pass"));  // false
	
	
	Interview Questions on BiPredicate
	----------------------------------
	Q1. What is the difference between Predicate and BiPredicate?
	A1. Predicate takes one argument and returns boolean, BiPredicate takes two arguments and returns boolean.
	
	Q2. Can you combine multiple BiPredicates?
	A2. Yes, using and(), or(), and negate() methods.
	
	Q3. Give a real-world scenario where BiPredicate is useful.
	A3. Validating login credentials (username, password), comparing two objects, filtering data with two inputs.
	
	Q4. Is BiPredicate a functional interface?
	A4. Yes, it has a single abstract method test(T t, U u), so it can be used with lambda expressions.
	
	Q5. Can BiPredicate be used with streams?
	A5. Yes, often in filter() when two parameters are required, though more commonly Predicate is used.
	    BiPredicate can be adapted using custom logic.
	
